h Nbd2) allows Black to exchange on c4 at their leisure or maintain pressure. Initiating the exchange with B: c4-a6 is the most promising option. It forces Black to recapture, likely with the Knight (...Nxa6), which places the Black Knight on a poor square at the edge of the board. This sequence simplifies the position (beneficial when up material), removes the annoying bishop preventing castling, and maintains White's advantage.\n\nReason: The Bishop on c4 is attacked. Moving it to a6 trades pieces, which favors White who is up a pawn. It also forces the Black Knight to a passive square (a6) and clears any potential control Black had over the f1 square, allowing White to castle safely on the next turn.\n\nAnswer: B: c4-a6"}
