night on c3, creating unnecessary counterplay.\n2.  **P: b2-b3**: Advances the pawn to avoid capture. If Black responds with `...a2` (threatening promotion), White can simply play `R: c1-a1` (blockading the pawn) or `N: c3-b1`/`N: c3xa2`. This keeps the position closed, keeps Black's Rook passive on a6, and neutralizes the pawn threat without conceding piece activity.\n\nGiven the large material lead, the safest path that minimizes opponent counterplay is best. `P: b2-b3` creates a solid defensive setup on the queenside, allowing White to proceed with converting the advantage on the rest of the board.\n\nReason: This move neutralizes Black's only immediate threat (the a3 pawn capturing on b2) while keeping the queenside lines closed to prevent Black's Rook from becoming active. It preserves White's winning advantage safely.\n\nAnswer: P: b2-b3"}
